WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.023
A priority queue looks like a queue

00:00:03.023 --> 00:00:05.670
elements go in, elements come out.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.166
But each element has a priority

00:00:08.166 --> 00:00:09.300
here a number.

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:12.302
The element that comes out is always

00:00:12.376 --> 00:00:14.881
the one with the highest priority

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:17.239
not the one that arrived first.

00:00:17.239 --> 00:00:20.333
So a priority queue does not follow FIFO.

00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:22.912
An emergency room works like this

00:00:22.912 --> 00:00:26.006
the most urgent patient is treated first

00:00:26.006 --> 00:00:28.290
whatever the order of arrival.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.261
The elements must be comparable.

00:00:31.343 --> 00:00:34.373
Whether a small number or a large one

00:00:34.455 --> 00:00:37.404
counts as high priority is a choice

00:00:37.486 --> 00:00:39.779
a min-priority queue removes

00:00:39.861 --> 00:00:42.072
the smallest element first

00:00:42.072 --> 00:00:44.857
a max-priority queue the largest.

00:00:44.857 --> 00:00:47.396
In Java, add or offer inserts

00:00:47.396 --> 00:00:50.918
poll removes the highest-priority element

00:00:50.918 --> 00:00:52.720
and peek looks at it.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:54.925
The slides' example.

00:00:55.006 --> 00:00:58.093
Java's PriorityQueue is a min-priority

00:00:58.174 --> 00:01:01.749
queue by default, so the smallest number has

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:03.536
the highest priority.

00:01:03.536 --> 00:01:06.379
Add 8, 3, 22, 1

00:01:06.379 --> 00:01:09.060
14 and 4, in any order.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:12.510
poll removes 1, the smallest.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:16.710
Add 2: now 2 is the smallest element

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.610
so peek gives 2.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.262
And the next poll() removes it.

00:01:22.262 --> 00:01:23.400
Add 5.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.654
Polling until the queue

00:01:25.736 --> 00:01:29.047
is empty gives 3, 4, 5, 8

00:01:29.047 --> 00:01:30.868
14, 22.

00:01:30.868 --> 00:01:34.013
5 came out between 4 and 8

00:01:34.013 --> 00:01:36.910
it had the third-highest priority.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:41.577
The slides say the elements of a priority queue

00:01:41.664 --> 00:01:44.881
are in ascending or descending order.

00:01:44.881 --> 00:01:46.968
That is how it behaves

00:01:46.968 --> 00:01:48.881
not how it is stored.

00:01:48.881 --> 00:01:51.751
Add 5, 1, 4, 2, 3.

00:01:51.751 --> 00:01:55.316
Printing the queue gives 1, 2, 4

00:01:55.316 --> 00:01:57.490
5, 3: not sorted.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.902
Only poll and peek respect the priorities

00:02:00.902 --> 00:02:04.037
polling gives 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:02:04.037 --> 00:02:07.099
Java's PriorityQueue keeps a binary heap

00:02:07.099 --> 00:02:09.214
which is only partly sorted.

00:02:09.287 --> 00:02:11.838
And elements with equal priority do

00:02:11.911 --> 00:02:14.098
not come out in arrival order

00:02:14.171 --> 00:02:16.504
unless you add a sequence number

00:02:16.577 --> 00:02:18.400
to the priority yourself.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.059
How do we implement one?

00:02:21.059 --> 00:02:24.958
With an unsorted array, insert just appends

00:02:25.045 --> 00:02:27.817
but finding the highest priority

00:02:27.904 --> 00:02:29.983
means searching: O(n).

00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:32.929
Keep the elements sorted instead

00:02:32.929 --> 00:02:36.222
and removing the highest is O(1)

00:02:36.308 --> 00:02:38.561
but each insert must shift

00:02:38.648 --> 00:02:41.420
or walk to its position: O(n).

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:43.955
A balanced binary search tree

00:02:44.031 --> 00:02:45.990
gives O(log n) for both.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.929
The binary heap also gives O(log n)

00:02:48.929 --> 00:02:52.094
with peek in O(1), and it is simpler.

00:02:52.094 --> 00:02:55.410
Heaps are the subject of lecture seventeen.

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:58.882
Here is the sorted linked list

00:02:58.882 --> 00:03:01.960
taken literally from the slides

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:05.597
ascending order, smallest at the head.

00:03:05.597 --> 00:03:09.702
After the first poll, it holds 3, 4

00:03:09.702 --> 00:03:12.686
8, 14 and 22.

00:03:12.686 --> 00:03:13.619
Add 5.

00:03:13.619 --> 00:03:17.350
p starts at the head, and steps is 1.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:21.963
While p.next holds a value at most 5

00:03:21.963 --> 00:03:22.835
move on.

00:03:22.835 --> 00:03:26.905
4 is at most 5, so p moves to 4

00:03:26.905 --> 00:03:29.134
and steps becomes 2.

00:03:29.134 --> 00:03:33.300
8 is larger than 5: the walk stops.

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:37.585
p.next becomes a new node, holding 5

00:03:37.585 --> 00:03:41.251
whose next is the old p.next, 8.

00:03:41.251 --> 00:03:44.381
So add 5 walked past two nodes

00:03:44.381 --> 00:03:46.170
as the demo prints.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:49.303
Insert walks the list: O(n).

00:03:49.303 --> 00:03:53.200
But poll just unlinks the head: O(1).

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:56.078
The second relative is the

00:03:56.167 --> 00:03:59.526
double-ended queue, or Deque for short.

00:03:59.526 --> 00:04:01.912
It is a generalised queue

00:04:02.001 --> 00:04:04.475
elements can be inserted and

00:04:04.564 --> 00:04:06.420
deleted at both ends.

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:09.568
It does not enforce FIFO or LIFO

00:04:09.568 --> 00:04:11.094
it supports both.

00:04:11.094 --> 00:04:14.062
Insert and delete at the same end

00:04:14.062 --> 00:04:15.673
and it is a stack.

00:04:15.673 --> 00:04:19.319
Insert at one end and delete at the other

00:04:19.319 --> 00:04:20.930
and it is a queue.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:24.388
The slides name six operations

00:04:24.481 --> 00:04:27.267
enqueue front and enqueue rear

00:04:27.359 --> 00:04:29.309
insert at either end

00:04:29.402 --> 00:04:32.187
dequeue front and dequeue rear

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:34.230
delete at either end.

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:37.039
And get front and get rear just look.

00:04:37.106 --> 00:04:38.900
Java's Deque interface names

00:04:38.967 --> 00:04:40.629
them with first and last

00:04:40.629 --> 00:04:43.420
as in the right-hand column of the table.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.364
A Deque fits a circular array perfectly.

00:04:47.364 --> 00:04:49.951
Forward is (i + 1) mod n

00:04:49.951 --> 00:04:52.193
as in the circular queue.

00:04:52.193 --> 00:04:54.867
The new direction is backward

00:04:54.867 --> 00:04:57.195
(i - 1 + n) mod n.

00:04:57.282 --> 00:04:59.869
Adding n first keeps the index

00:04:59.955 --> 00:05:01.680
from going negative.

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:05.669
Our class keeps front, rear and size.

00:05:05.767 --> 00:05:08.226
It starts with front 0

00:05:08.324 --> 00:05:11.471
and rear n - 1 (4 here)

00:05:11.569 --> 00:05:14.028
so that rear equals (front

00:05:14.126 --> 00:05:16.782
+ size - 1) mod n

00:05:16.782 --> 00:05:19.830
holds even for the empty Deque.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:24.275
Enqueue rear moves rear forward and stores.

00:05:24.275 --> 00:05:28.561
Dequeue front reads and moves front forward.

00:05:28.561 --> 00:05:32.561
Enqueue front moves front backward first

00:05:32.561 --> 00:05:36.180
and dequeue rear moves rear backward.

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:40.272
The slides' first picture, with n = 5.

00:05:40.272 --> 00:05:41.893
Enqueue rear 1

00:05:41.893 --> 00:05:44.595
rear moves forward from 4

00:05:44.595 --> 00:05:48.467
wrapping to 0, and 1 is stored there.

00:05:48.467 --> 00:05:51.799
Enqueue rear 2: rear moves to 1.

00:05:51.799 --> 00:05:54.320
And 3 goes to cell 2.

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:57.961
Enqueue front 5: front was 0

00:05:57.961 --> 00:06:01.817
and 0 - 1 is not a valid index

00:06:01.817 --> 00:06:03.792
so it wraps to 4.

00:06:03.792 --> 00:06:06.425
5 goes in the last cell.

00:06:06.425 --> 00:06:08.587
Now get front is 5

00:06:08.587 --> 00:06:10.750
and get rear is 3.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.547
Dequeue front returns 5

00:06:13.547 --> 00:06:16.720
and front moves forward from 4

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:18.804
wrapping back to 0.

00:06:18.804 --> 00:06:21.342
Dequeue rear returns 3

00:06:21.342 --> 00:06:23.880
and rear moves back to 1.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:26.780
Every one of these is O(1).

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:30.973
The second picture: enqueue rear 1

00:06:30.973 --> 00:06:33.512
then enqueue front 3

00:06:33.512 --> 00:06:35.856
then enqueue front 2.

00:06:35.856 --> 00:06:40.250
Read from the front, the Deque is 2, 3,

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:42.887
1: front at index 3

00:06:42.887 --> 00:06:44.840
rear at index 0.

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:47.226
Dequeue rear returns 1

00:06:47.226 --> 00:06:50.075
and rear moves backward from 0

00:06:50.075 --> 00:06:51.541
wrapping to 4.

00:06:51.541 --> 00:06:53.169
Without the plus n

00:06:53.169 --> 00:06:56.670
0 - 1 would be -1 in Java.

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:58.162
Your turn.

00:06:58.162 --> 00:07:02.268
Start again from the empty Deque with n = 5

00:07:02.268 --> 00:07:04.366
front 0, rear 4.

00:07:04.366 --> 00:07:08.472
Do enqueue front 7, enqueue front 8

00:07:08.472 --> 00:07:10.570
and enqueue rear 9.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:14.315
Where is each number, and what are f and r?

00:07:14.315 --> 00:07:16.920
Pause the video and work it out.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:22.253
Enqueue front 7:

00:07:22.253 --> 00:07:25.320
front goes from 0 back to 4

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:27.686
so 7 is at index 4.

00:07:27.686 --> 00:07:31.366
Enqueue front 8: front goes to 3

00:07:31.366 --> 00:07:33.820
so 8 is at index 3.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:37.532
Enqueue rear 9: rear goes from 4

00:07:37.532 --> 00:07:40.377
where 7 is, forward to 0

00:07:40.377 --> 00:07:42.619
so 9 is at index 0.

00:07:42.619 --> 00:07:46.153
The cells are 9, empty, empty, 8

00:07:46.153 --> 00:07:48.912
7, with f 3 and r 0.

00:07:48.912 --> 00:07:52.533
From the front, the Deque is 8, 7

00:07:52.533 --> 00:07:53.050
9.

00:07:53.630 --> 00:07:54.554
In practice,

00:07:54.554 --> 00:07:57.170
use java.util.ArrayDeque,

00:07:57.247 --> 00:07:59.864
a circular-array Deque that doubles

00:07:59.941 --> 00:08:01.942
its array when it is full.

00:08:02.019 --> 00:08:04.097
Java recommends it for both

00:08:04.174 --> 00:08:07.560
stacks and queues, rather than the old Stack

00:08:07.637 --> 00:08:09.407
class or a linked list.

00:08:09.407 --> 00:08:12.870
Push works at one end only: it is addFirst.

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:16.965
offerLast puts each number in at the rear.

00:08:16.965 --> 00:08:20.622
Popping the stack gives 4, 3, 2

00:08:20.622 --> 00:08:21.580
1: LIFO.

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:26.064
Polling the queue from the front gives 1

00:08:26.064 --> 00:08:28.371
2, 3, 4: FIFO.

00:08:28.371 --> 00:08:32.312
And a third Deque, filled from both ends

00:08:32.312 --> 00:08:33.850
prints a, b, c.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:36.832
Every Deque operation on a circular

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:39.541
array is O(1); for ArrayDeque

00:08:39.541 --> 00:08:41.089
O(1) amortised,

00:08:41.089 --> 00:08:43.720
counting the occasional doubling.

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:45.081
To sum up.

00:08:45.081 --> 00:08:48.361
1: A priority queue removes the element

00:08:48.439 --> 00:08:50.938
with the highest priority first,

00:08:50.938 --> 00:08:52.500
not the oldest one.

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:56.595
2: Java's PriorityQueue is a min-heap.

00:08:56.595 --> 00:09:00.152
poll and peek respect the priorities;

00:09:00.152 --> 00:09:01.930
printing does not.

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:02.809
3:

00:09:02.809 --> 00:09:06.419
A Deque inserts and removes at both ends.

00:09:06.507 --> 00:09:08.708
On a circular array, every

00:09:08.796 --> 00:09:10.734
operation is O(1),

00:09:10.734 --> 00:09:14.344
and stepping back is (i - 1 + n)

00:09:14.344 --> 00:09:14.960
mod n.

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:16.148
Try this.

00:09:16.148 --> 00:09:19.219
Write isPalindrome with a Deque

00:09:19.219 --> 00:09:21.732
put all the characters in

00:09:21.825 --> 00:09:24.710
then repeatedly remove one from

00:09:24.804 --> 00:09:26.758
each end and compare.

00:09:26.758 --> 00:09:29.550
What do "level" and "queue" give?

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:31.027
In part four

00:09:31.027 --> 00:09:33.800
we build a stack out of queues

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:36.140
and a queue out of stacks.

